    "bog": {
      "word": "BOG",
      "definitions": [
        {
          "text": "A quagmire filled with decayed moss and other vegetable matter; wet spongy ground where a heavy body is apt to sink; a marsh; a morass. Appalled with thoughts of bog, or caverned pit, Of treacherous earth, subsiding where they tread. R. Jago.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "A little elevated spot or clump of earth, roots, and grass, in a marsh or swamp. [Local, U. S.] Bog bean. See Buck bean. bluiter, Bog jumper, the bittern. [Prov.] -- Bog butter, a hydrocarbon of butterlike consistence found in the peat bogs of Ireland. partially decomposed vegetable fiber. P. Cyc. a variety of brown iron ore, or limonite. (b) Bog manganese, the hydrated peroxide of manganese.",
          "partOfSpeech": "noun"
        },
        {
          "text": "To sink, as into a bog; to submerge in a bog; to cause to sink and stick, as in mud and mire. At another time, he was bogged up to the middle in the slough of Lochend. Sir W. Scott.",
          "partOfSpeech": "verb"
        }
      ],
